ible the ethical argument conveyed in them, and having exhibited what the Gnostic is in his life, proceed to show the philosophers that he is by no"],["Gregory Nazianzen",1,["Select Orations"],"XXVII.  Do not disdain to be baptized with a poor man, if you are rich; or if you are noble, with one who is lowborn; or if you are a master, with one who is up to the present time your slave.  Not even so will you be humbling yourself as Christ, unto Whom you are baptized today,"]]}},"c":[]}